1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:02,693
In November 2025,

2
00:00:02,693 --> 00:00:06,318
Microsoft's CEO said something strange.

3
00:00:06,568 --> 00:00:09,166
His company may have a bunch of AI chips

4
00:00:09,166 --> 00:00:12,289
sitting in inventory — that it can't plug in.

5
00:00:12,539 --> 00:00:14,258
The chips weren't the problem.

6
00:00:14,258 --> 00:00:16,577
The problem, in his words, was power.

7
00:00:16,827 --> 00:00:18,401
So here's the question:

8
00:00:18,401 --> 00:00:21,475
will electricity become the ceiling on AI?

9
00:00:21,775 --> 00:00:24,934
My answer is yes. But not in the way you think.

10
00:00:25,334 --> 00:00:27,561
Because the thing that's really running

11
00:00:27,561 --> 00:00:29,816
out might not be electricity at all.

12
00:00:30,616 --> 00:00:32,091
First, why does AI need

13
00:00:32,091 --> 00:00:34,792
so much electricity in the first place?

14
00:00:35,042 --> 00:00:37,241
Data centers are built from racks,

15
00:00:37,241 --> 00:00:39,770
tall cabinets packed with computers.

16
00:00:40,020 --> 00:00:44,304
In 2024, the average rack drew under 8 kilowatts.

17
00:00:44,554 --> 00:00:48,123
A single rack of Nvidia's GB200 AI

18
00:00:48,123 --> 00:00:52,417
system is estimated to draw around 120.

19
00:00:52,667 --> 00:00:56,196
That's roughly fifteen times as much, in one cabinet.

20
00:00:56,446 --> 00:00:58,851
Now fill a whole campus with them.

21
00:00:58,851 --> 00:01:03,025
Elon Musk's xAI already runs about one and a half gigawatts

22
00:01:03,025 --> 00:01:07,186
of its own power plants for its data centers in Memphis.

23
00:01:07,436 --> 00:01:10,481
One gigawatt, running around the clock,

24
00:01:10,481 --> 00:01:14,507
is about what 800,000 average American homes use.

25
00:01:14,757 --> 00:01:19,209
Add it all up, and in 2024, data centers worldwide

26
00:01:19,209 --> 00:01:23,416
used about 415 terawatt-hours of electricity.

27
00:01:23,666 --> 00:01:25,818
That's around one and a half percent

28
00:01:25,818 --> 00:01:28,290
of all the electricity the world uses.

29
00:01:28,890 --> 00:01:31,867
One and a half percent doesn't sound like a crisis.

30
00:01:32,117 --> 00:01:35,901
But the International Energy Agency expects

31
00:01:35,901 --> 00:01:41,468
it to more than double by 2030, to around 945 terawatt-hours.

32
00:01:41,718 --> 00:01:44,895
That's slightly more than all of Japan uses today.

33
00:01:45,145 --> 00:01:49,272
And it's concentrated. The United States alone accounts

34
00:01:49,272 --> 00:01:53,074
for about 45 percent of data center electricity.

35
00:01:53,324 --> 00:01:56,710
Through 2030, data centers are expected to drive

36
00:01:56,710 --> 00:02:00,719
nearly half of America's growth in electricity demand.

37
00:02:01,519 --> 00:02:04,182
You might think: chips keep getting more efficient.

38
00:02:04,182 --> 00:02:05,426
Won't that solve it?

39
00:02:05,676 --> 00:02:08,013
They are getting more efficient.

40
00:02:08,013 --> 00:02:11,445
Nvidia says its newest system delivers up to 25

41
00:02:11,445 --> 00:02:14,367
times more performance at the same power

42
00:02:14,367 --> 00:02:18,218
than the previous generation, on certain AI tasks.

43
00:02:18,468 --> 00:02:21,256
And yet, data center electricity use

44
00:02:21,256 --> 00:02:24,741
has grown about 12 percent a year since 2017,

45
00:02:24,741 --> 00:02:29,356
more than four times faster than electricity use overall.

46
00:02:29,606 --> 00:02:32,948
This is an old story. In 1865, the economist

47
00:02:32,948 --> 00:02:37,201
William Stanley Jevons noticed that more efficient steam

48
00:02:37,201 --> 00:02:41,730
engines didn't cut Britain's coal use. They increased it.

49
00:02:41,980 --> 00:02:44,149
When something gets cheaper to use,

50
00:02:44,149 --> 00:02:46,332
we find far more ways to use it.

51
00:02:46,582 --> 00:02:48,412
Cheaper AI means more AI.

52
00:02:48,412 --> 00:02:52,857
Efficiency doesn't shrink the bill. It grows the appetite.

53
00:02:53,657 --> 00:02:55,396
Here's what most people miss.

54
00:02:55,996 --> 00:02:58,521
The world isn't running out of electricity.

55
00:02:58,521 --> 00:03:01,012
It's running out of time to connect it.

56
00:03:01,262 --> 00:03:03,498
A data center is just a building.

57
00:03:03,498 --> 00:03:06,546
Feeding it takes a whole chain: power plants,

58
00:03:06,546 --> 00:03:08,982
transmission lines, transformers.

59
00:03:09,232 --> 00:03:12,187
And every link in that chain has a waiting line.

60
00:03:12,437 --> 00:03:14,948
Start with power plants. In the US,

61
00:03:14,948 --> 00:03:18,966
a new power project has to apply to connect to the grid,

62
00:03:18,966 --> 00:03:20,529
then wait its turn.

63
00:03:20,779 --> 00:03:24,225
At the end of 2025, more than 2,000 gigawatts

64
00:03:24,225 --> 00:03:28,560
of generation and storage were sitting in those lines.

65
00:03:28,810 --> 00:03:32,227
For projects that finally came online in 2025,

66
00:03:32,227 --> 00:03:34,678
the typical wait from application

67
00:03:34,678 --> 00:03:37,924
to switching on was more than five years.

68
00:03:38,174 --> 00:03:40,489
And most never make it at all.

69
00:03:40,489 --> 00:03:44,271
Of everything that applied between 2000 and 2020,

70
00:03:44,271 --> 00:03:46,863
only 13 percent was ever built.

71
00:03:47,113 --> 00:03:49,345
New transmission lines in advanced

72
00:03:49,345 --> 00:03:52,433
economies take four to eight years to build.

73
00:03:52,683 --> 00:03:55,390
Large power transformers now have lead

74
00:03:55,390 --> 00:03:57,242
times of up to four years,

75
00:03:57,242 --> 00:04:01,432
and prices have jumped roughly 80 percent in five years.

76
00:04:01,682 --> 00:04:03,880
Gas turbines? GE Vernova,

77
00:04:03,880 --> 00:04:06,166
one of the biggest makers,

78
00:04:06,166 --> 00:04:11,289
said in December 2025 that it was sold out through 2028.

79
00:04:11,539 --> 00:04:15,545
The IEA estimates that, unless these bottlenecks are fixed,

80
00:04:15,545 --> 00:04:17,785
around 20 percent of planned data

81
00:04:17,785 --> 00:04:20,226
center projects could be delayed.

82
00:04:20,476 --> 00:04:25,531
In September 2026, Morgan Stanley put a number on the gap:

83
00:04:25,531 --> 00:04:28,407
a shortfall of about 32 gigawatts

84
00:04:28,407 --> 00:04:31,483
for US data centers through 2028.

85
00:04:31,733 --> 00:04:34,049
That's roughly a third of what they need.

86
00:04:34,299 --> 00:04:36,351
Chips move on product cycles.

87
00:04:36,351 --> 00:04:39,607
Power moves on permit and construction cycles,

88
00:04:39,607 --> 00:04:42,213
and those run five years and more.

89
00:04:43,013 --> 00:04:45,805
When something is scarce, its price tells you.

90
00:04:46,055 --> 00:04:49,757
PJM runs the power grid across all or parts

91
00:04:49,757 --> 00:04:53,057
of 13 US states and Washington, D.C.

92
00:04:53,307 --> 00:04:53,995
Every year,

93
00:04:53,995 --> 00:04:56,947
it holds an auction for future power supply.

94
00:04:57,197 --> 00:04:58,884
For 2024 to 2025,

95
00:04:58,884 --> 00:05:03,846
the price was about 29 dollars per megawatt-day.

96
00:05:04,096 --> 00:05:08,111
For 2027 to 2028, it hit 333 dollars,

97
00:05:08,111 --> 00:05:11,566
the maximum the rules allowed.

98
00:05:11,816 --> 00:05:14,582
Analysts estimate that without the cap,

99
00:05:14,582 --> 00:05:17,051
it would have been close to 530.

100
00:05:17,301 --> 00:05:18,712
And even at that price,

101
00:05:18,712 --> 00:05:22,286
the auction still fell short of its reliability target.

102
00:05:22,536 --> 00:05:28,428
The reason? PJM's demand forecast jumped by 5,250 megawatts,

103
00:05:28,428 --> 00:05:32,556
almost entirely because of data centers.

104
00:05:32,806 --> 00:05:35,455
And that cost doesn't stay with tech companies.

105
00:05:35,705 --> 00:05:37,379
In Washington, D.C.,

106
00:05:37,379 --> 00:05:41,563
one utility's residential customers saw bills rise

107
00:05:41,563 --> 00:05:45,496
about 21 dollars a month starting in June 2025,

108
00:05:45,496 --> 00:05:49,294
around 10 of it from these capacity prices.

109
00:05:50,094 --> 00:05:52,058
So the tech giants stopped waiting.

110
00:05:52,308 --> 00:05:56,204
Microsoft signed a deal to bring back a reactor

111
00:05:56,204 --> 00:06:00,016
at Three Mile Island, which shut down in 2019.

112
00:06:00,016 --> 00:06:03,366
It's now expected back online in 2027.

113
00:06:03,616 --> 00:06:07,123
Google signed up for small nuclear reactors

114
00:06:07,123 --> 00:06:11,691
from a company called Kairos Power: up to 500 megawatts,

115
00:06:11,691 --> 00:06:16,295
with the first targeted for 2030 and the rest by 2035.

116
00:06:16,545 --> 00:06:18,791
Notice the dates. Even the fixes run

117
00:06:18,791 --> 00:06:21,236
on the grid's clock, not the chip's.

118
00:06:21,486 --> 00:06:23,751
Others are skipping the grid entirely.

119
00:06:24,001 --> 00:06:27,700
One analysis counted 59 US data center projects

120
00:06:27,700 --> 00:06:32,229
planning about 90 gigawatts of their own on-site power.

121
00:06:32,479 --> 00:06:34,844
That's more than a quarter of all planned

122
00:06:34,844 --> 00:06:37,120
data center capacity in the country.

123
00:06:37,370 --> 00:06:39,728
And 92 percent of those projects

124
00:06:39,728 --> 00:06:42,801
were announced since the start of 2025.

125
00:06:43,201 --> 00:06:46,077
But here's the catch. As of mid-2026,

126
00:06:46,077 --> 00:06:50,118
only about 2 gigawatts of that was actually running.

127
00:06:50,118 --> 00:06:51,717
Roughly 2 percent.

128
00:06:51,967 --> 00:06:54,065
Announcing a power plant is easy.

129
00:06:54,065 --> 00:06:56,235
Building one is the bottleneck.

130
00:06:56,485 --> 00:07:00,044
It got strange. In December 2025, Crusoe,

131
00:07:00,044 --> 00:07:03,343
a company that builds AI data centers,

132
00:07:03,343 --> 00:07:07,250
ordered 29 gas turbines from Boom Supersonic,

133
00:07:07,250 --> 00:07:11,877
a startup best known for designing supersonic jets.

134
00:07:12,127 --> 00:07:13,994
Then, in September 2026,

135
00:07:13,994 --> 00:07:17,416
Crusoe walked away from that deal and turned

136
00:07:17,416 --> 00:07:20,805
to established suppliers like GE Vernova.

137
00:07:21,055 --> 00:07:23,016
When time is the bottleneck,

138
00:07:23,016 --> 00:07:26,368
an untested machine is a risk few can afford.

139
00:07:27,168 --> 00:07:28,461
Now flip the map.

140
00:07:28,711 --> 00:07:30,893
China generates more than twice

141
00:07:30,893 --> 00:07:33,980
as much electricity as the United States.

142
00:07:34,230 --> 00:07:37,630
In 2025 alone, it added more than 430

143
00:07:37,630 --> 00:07:41,231
gigawatts of wind and solar capacity.

144
00:07:41,481 --> 00:07:44,108
BloombergNEF expects China to add

145
00:07:44,108 --> 00:07:47,133
more than six times as much generation

146
00:07:47,133 --> 00:07:50,836
capacity as the US over the next five years.

147
00:07:51,086 --> 00:07:54,244
One business school researcher put it simply:

148
00:07:54,244 --> 00:07:57,262
the US has the chips and is short on power.

149
00:07:57,262 --> 00:08:00,410
China has the power and is short on chips.

150
00:08:00,660 --> 00:08:02,725
So electricity isn't one ceiling.

151
00:08:02,725 --> 00:08:06,367
It's a different ceiling, depending on where you stand.

152
00:08:07,167 --> 00:08:08,618
So here's my call.

153
00:08:08,868 --> 00:08:10,644
Electricity won't stop AI.

154
00:08:10,644 --> 00:08:14,124
Too much money is chasing this problem for that.

155
00:08:14,374 --> 00:08:16,212
But for the next few years,

156
00:08:16,212 --> 00:08:19,617
power will decide three things: how fast AI grows,

157
00:08:19,617 --> 00:08:22,132
where it gets built, and who wins.

158
00:08:22,382 --> 00:08:25,536
The winners won't just be the ones with the best models.

159
00:08:25,536 --> 00:08:28,327
They'll be the ones who locked in power early.

160
00:08:28,577 --> 00:08:31,084
I'd go further: by the end of this decade,

161
00:08:31,084 --> 00:08:33,532
a guaranteed grid connection may be worth

162
00:08:33,532 --> 00:08:35,941
more than the chips you plug into it.

163
00:08:36,341 --> 00:08:39,283
And if I'm wrong, it'll be because the grid

164
00:08:39,283 --> 00:08:42,635
suddenly started moving at chip speed: new lines,

165
00:08:42,635 --> 00:08:46,329
plants and transformers arriving in two or three years

166
00:08:46,329 --> 00:08:50,087
instead of five or more. That's the signal to watch.

167
00:08:50,887 --> 00:08:52,478
What does this mean for you?

168
00:08:52,728 --> 00:08:55,490
If you live where data centers are booming,

169
00:08:55,490 --> 00:08:59,416
part of AI's bill may already be on your electricity bill.

170
00:08:59,666 --> 00:09:03,185
And next time someone says AI is limited by chips,

171
00:09:03,185 --> 00:09:05,227
remember Microsoft's problem.

172
00:09:05,227 --> 00:09:08,172
The chips were there. The power wasn't.

173
00:09:08,422 --> 00:09:11,141
So which do you think runs out first: chips,

174
00:09:11,141 --> 00:09:13,812
or electricity? Tell me in the comments.
